.TH cmp "" "Command"
.SH NAME
cmp \- compare bytes of two files
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBcmp [\-l] [\-s] \fIfile1 file2\fB [\fIskip1 skip2\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
cmp -- compare bytes of two files
Usage:	cmp [ -ls ] file1 file2 [ skip1 skip2 ]
Options:
	-l	Print byte number and the bytes at each difference
	-s	Return status (do not print anything)
If 'file1' is '-', use standard input.
If 'skip1' and 'skip2' are present, they are the number of bytes
to skip before comparing in 'file1' and 'file2', respectively.
.HE
.B cmp
compares
.I file1
and
.I file2
byte by byte.
.I file1
may be
.B \-
to mean the standard input.
.PP
Without
.BR \-l ,
.B cmp
stops at the first byte that differs and prints the byte number
(counting from 1) and line number (counting newlines seen in
.IR file1 )
at which the files first differ, then exits.
If one file ends before the other,
.B cmp
reports which file hit end of file first.
.PP
If
.I skip1
and
.I skip2
are given, they are decimal byte counts;
.B cmp
skips that many bytes into
.I file1
and
.I file2
respectively before starting the comparison.
.IP \fB\-l\fR
Do not stop at the first difference.
Instead, for every byte at which the files differ, print the byte
number and the octal value of the differing byte from each file.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
Print nothing; only set the exit status.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR comm ,
.B diff
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B cmp
exits 0 if the files are identical, 1 if they differ, and 2 if a
file cannot be opened (this happens regardless of
.BR \-s ,
though the message is suppressed by
.BR \-s ).
